  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Today, daytime heating of the airmass in the \nvicinity of the seabreeze and any residual boundaries from early\nmorning convection should support a renewed risk of \nthunderstorms. Once again, some of these could be strong to \nmarginally severe. Residually dry air aloft should support DCAPE\nvalues of 1000+ j/kg. This in tandem with steep low-level lapse\nrates and moderate instability should lead to the primary risk \nbeing damaging winds of 40-60 mph. Forecast aids like the \nECMWF's EFI show a decent environmental signal for a few severe \nthunderstorms which is also supported by short-range ensemble \nguidance. The convective potential this afternoon should \nprimarily be diurnally-driven, with the risk decreasing after \nsunset.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An anomalously strong upper level trough is \nforecast to develop over the Mid-Atlantic Region on Tuesday, \nwith a potent shortwave diving southeast through Virginia and \nthe Carolinas Tuesday into Tuesday night. A strong LLJ ahead of \nthis feature should lead to increasing moisture advection, with \nPWATs once again climbing back above 2\". Meanwhile, heating of a\nmoist boundary layer should support moderate instability, with \nMLCAPE rising into the 1000-1500j/kg range. Aloft, a notable \nupper jet max is forecast to overspread the instability axis, \nsupporting deep layer shear of 30-45kt. While instability will \nbe moderate, the shear/instability combo is notable for this \ntime of year, as is the amount of lift expected. \n\nSynoptically, the combination of all of the factors above \nchecks several boxes for severe weather potential. This also \nhas overwhelming support from ensemble, analog, and machine \nlearning guidance. The main potential limiting factor is timing.\nSome guidance suggests convection may not reach ENC until after\nsunset. Despite the potential for a gradually stabilizing \nboundary layer, the strong large-scale forcing should help \nsupport deep convection well past the typical diurnal max in \nconvective intensity this time of year. Given the amount of \nforcing present, it appears likely that an MCS will move through\nENC during the evening and early overnight hours posing a risk \nof damaging winds. Low-level SRH around 100m2/s2 may support a \nfew rotating updrafts capable of hail and perhaps a brief \ntornado. However, those latter 2 hazards should be lower \ncompared to the wind risk. Lastly, while a MCS would be more \nprogressive, the high PWAT airmass should support heavy rainfall\nrates which may support a short-fused flooding risk, especially\nfor the area hardest hit the past few days (ie. Hyde County \neast through the Outer Banks).\n\nAhead of the convection, hot and humid conditions are expected,\nwith heat indices rising to 100-105 degrees. This appears to be\na bit marginal for heat headlines, but is something we'll \ncontinue to assess in later forecasts.",
